According to the question, Before stretching, \[A=A_1,\qquad l=l_1,\qquad R=R_1\] Since volume remains constant, \[V=A\,l=A_1l_1 \tag{i}\] After stretching, \[l_2=\alpha l\] Let the new area be \(A_2\). Then, \[V=A_2l_2=A_2(\alpha l) \tag{ii}\] From (i) and (ii), \[A\,l=A_2(\alpha l)\] \[A_2=\frac{A}{\alpha}\] Since \[R=\rho\frac{l}{A}\] the original resistance is \[R_1=R=\rho\frac{l}{A} \tag{iii}\] The new resistance is \[R_2=\rho\frac{l_2}{A_2}
=\rho\frac{\alpha l}{A/\alpha}
=\alpha^2\frac{\rho l}{A}\] Using (iii), \[R_2=\alpha^2R\] \[\boxed{\text{B) $\alpha^2 R$}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>For uniform stretching of a wire with constant volume and unchanged resistivity, resistance varies as the square of its length, i.e., \(R \propto l^2\).</li></ul></div>

According to the question, \[v(t)=v_1(t)+v_2(t)\] where, <img src="img/14f714ce3b57d6e9.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,256px)"> The resultant waveform alternates between \(4\,\text{V}\) and \(0\,\text{V}\) with equal time duration. \[V_{\mathrm{rms}}
=
\sqrt{\frac{1}{T}
\left[
\int_0^{T/2}(4)^2\,dt
+
\int_{T/2}^{T}0\,dt
\right]}\] \[V_{\mathrm{rms}}
=
\sqrt{\frac{16}{T}\int_0^{T/2}dt}
=
\sqrt{\frac{16}{T}\left[\frac{T}{2}\right]}
=
\sqrt{8}\,\text{V}\] \[\boxed{\text{C) $\sqrt{8}\,\text{V}$}}\]

Given: \[x(t)=\cos(1.2\pi t)+\cos(2\pi t)+\cos(2.8\pi t)\] \[\omega_1=1.2\pi~\text{rad/sec}
=
\frac{6\pi}{5}~\text{rad/sec}\] \[\omega_2=2\pi~\text{rad/sec}\] \[\omega_3=2.8\pi~\text{rad/sec}
=
\frac{14\pi}{5}~\text{rad/sec}\] Fundamental angular frequency is given by, \[\omega_0=\mathrm{HCF}(\omega_1,\omega_2,\omega_3)\] \[\omega_0
=
\mathrm{HCF}
\left(
\frac{6\pi}{5},
\frac{2\pi}{1},
\frac{14\pi}{5}
\right)\] \[\omega_0
=
\frac{\mathrm{HCF}[6\pi,2\pi,14\pi]}
{\mathrm{LCM}[5,1,5]}\] \[\omega_0=\frac{2\pi}{5}~\text{rad/sec}\] \[f_0
=
\frac{\omega_0}{2\pi}
=
\frac{\frac{2\pi}{5}}{2\pi}
=
\frac{1}{5}
=
0.2~\text{Hz}\] Hence, the correct option is (A). (A) 0.2 Hz

Given: \(2\,\text{V}\) DC voltage and \(4\,\text{V}\) peak-to-peak square wave signal are superimposed i.e. <img src="img/06bfb31f65df2b9d.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,320px)"> RMS value of superimposed signal is given by, \[v_{\mathrm{rms}}
=
\sqrt{
\frac{1}{T}
\int_{-T/2}^{T/2}
v^2(t)\,dt
}\] \[v_{\mathrm{rms}}
=
\sqrt{
\frac{1}{T}
\left[
\int_{-T/2}^{0}0\,dt
+
\int_{0}^{T/2}4^2\,dt
\right]
}\] \[v_{\mathrm{rms}}
=
\sqrt{
\frac{1}{T}
\left[
16\times\frac{T}{2}
\right]
}
=
\sqrt{8}\ \text{V}\] Hence, the correct option is (C). \((C)\ \sqrt{8}\,\text{V}\)

The output dc error is due to: <ol><li>Input offset voltage \(V_{i0}\),</li><li>Input bias current \(I_{b}\).</li></ol> The noise gain of the circuit is \[1+\frac{R_{f}}{R_{1}}
=1+\frac{100\,\text{k}\Omega}{100\,\text{k}\Omega}
=2.\] Hence, the output error due to offset voltage is \[V_{o,\text{offset}}
=2\times1\,\text{mV}
=2\,\text{mV}.\] The output error due to input bias current is \[V_{o,\text{bias}}
=I_{b}R_{f}
&lt;10\,\text{nA}\times100\,\text{k}\Omega
=1\,\text{mV}.\] Therefore, the maximum dc output error is \[V_{o,\max}
=2\,\text{mV}+1\,\text{mV}
=3\,\text{mV}.\] \[\boxed{3.0\,\text{mV}}\] \[\boxed{\text{(d) }3.0\,\text{mV}}\]

The circuit is a <strong>non-inverting programmable gain amplifier</strong>. Its gain is \[A_{v}=1+\frac{R_{f}}{R_{g}},\] When switch \(S_{2}\) is closed, <img src="img/599e246c75c4a848.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,224px)"> \[R_{f}=2\,\text{k}\Omega,\] \[R_{g}=1\,\text{k}\Omega+500\,\Omega+500\,\Omega
=2\,\text{k}\Omega.\] Therefore, \[A_{v}=1+\frac{2\,\text{k}\Omega}{2\,\text{k}\Omega}
=2.\] \[\boxed{\text{(b) }2}\]

The circuit is an <strong>inverting amplifier</strong> with \[R_{1}=1\,\text{k}\Omega,\qquad
R_{f}=10\,\text{k}\Omega.\] Hence, the closed-loop voltage gain is \[A_{v}=-\frac{R_{f}}{R_{1}}
=-\frac{10\,\text{k}\Omega}{1\,\text{k}\Omega}
=-10.\] The resistor \(R\) is connected from the non-inverting input to ground only to compensate the effect of input bias currents. It does <strong>not</strong> determine the closed-loop gain. Therefore, even if \(R\) is increased by \(50\%\), the voltage gain remains practically unchanged. \[\boxed{\text{Change in gain}\approx 0}\] \[\boxed{\text{(d) Negligible amount}}\]

From Q3.06.7, \[\frac{R_{1}}{R_{2}}=4.67.\] The threshold voltage at the non-inverting terminal is \[V_{+}
=
\frac{R_{2}}{R_{1}+R_{2}}V_{o}
+
\frac{R_{1}}{R_{1}+R_{2}}(1.5).\] For the upper trip point, the output is in the negative saturation state: \[V_{o}=-6.7\,\text{V}.\] Substituting, \[V_{UTP}
=
\frac{-6.7+4.67\times1.5}{4.67+1}
=
\frac{0.305}{5.67}
\approx2.42\,\text{V}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(c) }V_{UTP}=2.42\,\text{V}}\]

The circuit is a common-collector (emitter follower). Given, \[g_{m}=20\,\text{mS},\qquad
\beta_{0}=50,\qquad
r_{o}=\infty,\qquad
r_{b}=0.\] First, find the hybrid-\(\pi\) resistance: \[r_{\pi}=\frac{\beta_{0}}{g_{m}}
=\frac{50}{20\times10^{-3}}
=2.5\,\text{k}\Omega.\] The input resistance seen at the transistor base is \[R_{in}=r_{\pi}+(\beta_{0}+1)R_{E}
=2.5\,\text{k}\Omega+51(2\,\text{k}\Omega)
=104.5\,\text{k}\Omega.\] Hence, the base voltage is \[V_{b}
=V_{i}\frac{104.5}{104.5+1}
=0.9905\,V_{i}.\] The emitter follower gain from base to emitter is \[\frac{V_{o}}{V_{b}}
=\frac{(\beta_{0}+1)R_{E}}
{r_{\pi}+(\beta_{0}+1)R_{E}}
=\frac{102}{104.5}
=0.976.\] Therefore, \[A_{v}
=\frac{V_{o}}{V_{i}}
=0.9905\times0.976
\approx0.967.\] \[\boxed{\text{(a) }0.967}\]

For the given fixed-bias silicon transistor, \[I_B=\frac{V_{CC}-V_{BE}}{R_B}
=\frac{12-0.7}{100\,\text{k}\Omega}
=113\,\mu\text{A}\] The collector current is \[I_C=\beta I_B
=80\times113\,\mu\text{A}
=9.04\,\text{mA}\] The collector-to-emitter voltage is \[V_{CE}=V_{CC}-I_C R_C
=12-(9.04\,\text{mA})(2\,\text{k}\Omega)\] \[=12-18.08=-6.08\,\text{V}\] Since a negative value is not possible in the active region, the transistor operates in saturation. Therefore, \[V_{CE}\approx0.2\,\text{V}\] \[\boxed{\text{(b) }0.2\,\text{V}}\]

The open-loop transfer function is \[G(s)=\left(K_p+\frac{K_I}{s}\right)\frac{1}{(s+2)(s+10)}\] The characteristic equation of the closed-loop system is, \[1+G(s)=0\] \[1+\frac{K_ps+K_I}{s(s+2)(s+10)}=0\] \[s(s+2)(s+10)+K_ps+K_I=0\] \[s^3+12s^2+(20+K_p)s+K_I=0\] The Routh array is \[\begin{array}{c|cc}
s^3 &amp; 1 &amp; 20+K_p\\ s^2 &amp; 12 &amp; K_I\\ s^1 &amp; \dfrac{240+12K_p-K_I}{12} &amp; 0\\ s^0 &amp; K_I &amp;
\end{array}\] For stability, all the elements of the first column must be positive. So, \[240+12K_p-K_I&gt;0 \qquad \text{and} \qquad K_I&gt;0\] \(\qquad\) \(12K_p&gt;K_I-240\) \(\qquad \quad\) \(K_p&gt;\dfrac{K_I}{12}-20\) \[\boxed{(D)\;K_I&gt;0,\;K_p&gt;\dfrac{K_I}{12}-20}\]
